response:
 First, notice that whenever we partition the square into nine equal parts, we always remove precisely 5/9 of the area. In the limit, if infinitely many steps are  executed, the remaining area is precisely 0/9 = 0. This makes sense considering the area required to shade this figure. In the limit, every part of the square is shaded! [answer] So, the fractional part of the figure that is shaded is $\boxed{0}$. [/think]
There are numerous solutions. Here is one. Let $x = 1/3$ and write down the system \begin{align*}
x + 4(x^2 + 4x^4 + 16x^6 +...) &= 1\\
1 + 4x(1 + x^2x^2 + 4x^4x^4 + 16x^6x^6    +...) &= 1.
\end{align*}
Solving for the quadratic in $y = x + 4x^4+...$, we have
$y = 1/(2-4x) = 1/(1-x)$ so that
$x + 4x^4 + 16x^6 +... = 1/(1-x)$ and
$1+ 4xy + 16xy^2 +... = 1$.
[asy]
size(320,320);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
void drawSquares(int n){

draw((n,n)--(n,-n)--(-n,-n)--(-n,n)--cycle);

fill((-n,n)--(-x*n,n)--(-x*n,x*n)--(-n,x*n)--cycle);

fill((-n,-n)--(-x*n,-n)--(-x*n,-x*n)--(-n,-x*n)--cycle);

fill((n,-n)--(x*n,-n)--(x*n,-x*n)--(n,-x*n)--cycle);

fill((n,n)--(x*n,n)--(x*n,x*n)--(n,x*n)--cycle);
}

void loop(){
for(int i=0; i < 9; i++)
drawSquares(3^i);
}
loop()
[/asy]
In the limit as $n \to \infty$, we obtain
$1 + x y + xy^2+\dots + \cdots = 1$
which is true if and only if $xy = 0$. Since $x = 1/3$ is nonzero, we have $y = 0$ which would be true if $n$ were the inverse of $1-x$ or is to put it another way, if
$(1+x+x^2+x^3...)$ converges. Therefore,
